<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/foundational/the-neuron">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The brain's cell, the <strong>neuron</strong>, is an electrical device. Its membrane holds a
    resting charge of about <strong>−70 mV</strong>; when incoming signals push past a threshold
    (near −55 mV), the neuron fires an <strong>action potential</strong> — a self-propagating
    spike of ~+40 mV that races down its axon at up to 120 m/s. Everything you think, feel and
    remember is patterns of these spikes, passed across synapses in chemical Morse code.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one action potential, phase by phase — hover to read the voltage at each moment" widget="plotter" config={apCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve left to right: <strong>resting</strong> (−70 mV, maintained by the sodium–
    potassium pump), <strong>threshold</strong> crossed, <strong>depolarisation</strong> as sodium
    floods in, <strong>repolarisation</strong> as potassium flows out, a brief
    <strong>hyperpolarisation</strong> dip below rest, then recovery. The spike is all-or-nothing:
    stronger thoughts are not "bigger spikes" but <em>more frequent ones</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The parts and their jobs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dendrites</strong> — antennae collecting signals from thousands of other neurons.</li>
    <li><strong>Soma</strong> — the cell body, summing the inputs. Above threshold → spike.</li>
    <li><strong>Axon</strong> — the cable; myelin insulation lets signals jump along it (loss of myelin is what multiple sclerosis damages).</li>
    <li><strong>Synapse</strong> — the 20-nanometre gap where the spike triggers neurotransmitter release: glutamate to excite, GABA to quiet, dopamine for salience.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the all-or-nothing spike matters</h2>
  <p>
    Binary signalling makes the brain noise-resistant — like computer logic, information survives
    the journey. The <strong>rate code</strong> (how often a neuron fires) and the
    <strong>timing</strong> between spikes carry the message; a neuron firing at 100 Hz is
    "shouting". This electrical picture is also why anaesthetics, alcohol and antidepressants —
    all of which act on channels or synapses — change the mind: they are editing the spikes.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Hodgkin & Huxley, J. Physiol. (1952).</p>
</TopicPage>
